    "A": "Research storage and data movement in src/laplace_seq.c. Use pointer-swap candidate fcb1efc392aa4b52 as the composable foundation; its 0.061084 s is effectively tied with the more complicated pending-row candidate at 0.060763 s. Investigate contiguous, suitably aligned double-buffer storage with cached row pointers and valid restrict scopes. Measure layout changes separately. Preserve identical fixed boundaries in both buffers, including tiny grids where initial heat touches a boundary. Keep the pending-row algorithm as a standalone alternative: its in-place overwrite dependencies cannot be inserted directly into C's parallel row ownership.",
    "B": "Research the FP64 stencil kernel using 53fb04ccce554549, whose four independent NEON chains measured 0.063820 s versus 0.065049 s for the simpler vector kernel. Make the kernel compatible with ping-pong buffers and C's private current/next pointers, without retaining a copy-back pass. Preserve the original addition order, multiplication by 0.25, and comparison against the double-promoted 1.0e-5f threshold. Accumulate convergence masks privately per thread, reset them every sweep, and contribute their OR to C's convergence protocol. Retain guarded ARM64 intrinsics, bounded vector loads, scalar tails and a portable fallback. Measure whether four chains still help once threading and copy elimination are present.",
    "C": "Research threading overhead from leader 4ea2272b65094b10. Retain persistent row ownership, private pointer swaps and the one-barrier-per-sweep protocol. Preserve alternating padded flag banks: the barrier completes stencil accesses, while alternating banks prevent fast threads from overwriting votes still being read. Investigate source-level team sizing up to the four available cores and a general useful-work-based serial/parallel crossover; do not tune dispatch to individual test identities. Keep small or short workloads serial when measurements justify it, and preserve builds without OpenMP. Compare alternatives under the unchanged compiler and measurement configuration rather than assuming fewer synchronization operations always win.",
    "G": "Use 4ea2272b65094b10 at 0.047271 s as the integration anchor and retained fallback. Prioritize transplanting B's vector row kernel into its private-buffer loop; evaluate A's storage layout separately, then combine only if the available measurements justify it. Do not combine the pending-row overwrite algorithm with parallel sweeps without a separate dependency-safe design. Measure integrated programs end to end against the anchor using the unchanged oracle, tests and timing protocol; gains are not additive. Select the fastest validated complete program, including the unmodified anchor if integrations regress. Review zero-iteration behavior, empty interiors, odd/even buffer parity, boundary preservation and committing the convergence-triggering sweep. Preserve six-decimal output, trailing spaces and row newlines.",
    "analysis": "Only Problem E and src/laplace_seq.c are relevant; A/B/C are research-role names. Baseline time is 0.069839 s. The best supplied program, C's persistent-team ping-pong implementation, takes 0.047271 s: about 1.48x baseline speedup. C's earlier copy-based implementation takes 0.049233 s, while standalone storage and SIMD candidates take roughly 0.061–0.065 s. These aggregate measurements favor using C as the integration anchor, but do not isolate individual transformation gains or establish additive benefits. Correctness requires synchronous double-precision Jacobi updates, unchanged fixed boundaries, the original arithmetic order and float-rounded threshold, strict greater-than convergence comparisons, and committing every computed sweep before stopping. Initial central heat is not a continuously reapplied source."
